course: history
lesson: 47
title: Ukraine in the Second World War
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In 1939 Hitler and Stalin secretly split eastern Europe between them, and the Soviet Union took Galicia and Volhynia from Poland; the teacher used the old Soviet word «reunification», but Danylo's grandmother remembered arrests and deportations (people forced from their homes and sent far away), so for many families it was an occupation.
explain: From 1941 to 1944 Nazi Germany occupied Ukraine; millions of soldiers and civilians died, cities lay in ruins, and over two million people were taken to Germany as forced labourers, called Ostarbeiter, «eastern workers».
explain: At Babyn Yar, a ravine in Kyiv, Nazi forces murdered about 33,771 Jews in two days in September 1941, and tens of thousands more people were killed there later; it is first of all a place of the Holocaust, the Nazi murder of Europe's Jews, and today people come there quietly to remember.
explain: The war was not one story with heroes on one side: millions of Ukrainians fought in the Red Army, the UPA fought for independence but some of its units killed Polish civilians, and over 2,600 Ukrainians risked their lives to hide Jews, like a classmate who stands up for a bullied kid when everyone else is afraid.
explain: The wise lesson: say «among the hardest-hit» instead of ranking suffering, honour the brave by being honest about the others, and remember that one people's pain, like the Crimean Tatars' deportation, helps us understand another's.
fact: In September 1939, after the Molotov–Ribbentrop pact divided eastern Europe, the Soviet Union took eastern Poland, including Galicia and Volhynia, and deportations and arrests followed.
fact: Nazi Germany occupied Ukraine from 1941 to 1944; millions of civilians and soldiers died, and over two million people were taken to Germany for forced labour as «Ostarbeiter».
fact: At Babyn Yar in Kyiv, on 29–30 September 1941, Nazi forces murdered about 33,771 Jews; in the following years tens of thousands more were killed there, including Roma, prisoners of war and Ukrainian nationalists.
fact: Millions of Ukrainians fought in the Red Army; several fronts were named «Ukrainian fronts» after where they fought, and the victory came from all the Soviet peoples together with the Allies.
fact: The Ukrainian Insurgent Army (UPA) fought for independence against Soviet forces and at times against the Germans; in 1943–1944 UPA units carried out mass killings of Polish civilians in Volhynia, and Ukrainian and Polish historians work on these facts together.
fact: In May 1944 Stalin's regime deported the whole Crimean Tatar people to Central Asia; many died on the way and in the first years, and Ukraine recognises it as genocide.
fact: Jamala's song «1944», about the deportation of her Crimean Tatar family, won Eurovision for Ukraine in 2016.
fact: Yad Vashem, Israel's Holocaust memorial, has recognised over 2,600 Ukrainians as Righteous Among the Nations, non-Jews who risked their lives to save Jews; Metropolitan Andrey Sheptytsky's network hid Jewish children, while some other people collaborated (helped the Nazis) and most were too afraid to act.
fact: The Red Army crossed the wide Dnipro under fire and retook Kyiv on 6 November 1943 after heavy fighting; the city had lost much of its population.
fact: In 1945 Soviet Ukraine became one of the 51 founding members of the United Nations, with its own seat, though its foreign policy was controlled from Moscow.
fact: Soviet Ukraine suffered three famines: 1921–1923, 1932–1933 (the Holodomor) and 1946–1947, the last after drought and war damage, made worse by grain exports.
fact: In 1954 the Soviet government transferred Crimea from the Russian republic to the Ukrainian republic; in 1991 Crimea stayed in independent Ukraine, and Russia recognised Ukraine's borders in treaties of 1994 and 1997.
exercise: What did the Molotov–Ribbentrop pact divide? | Eastern Europe between Hitler and Stalin | Kyiv between two brothers | The old lands of the Zaporizhian Sich between rival hetmans | Crimea between Turkey and Greece
exercise: Which regions did the Soviet Union take from Poland in 1939? | Galicia and Volhynia | Crimea and the city of Odesa | Kyiv and the city of Kharkiv | Smolensk and the city of Moscow
exercise: When did Nazi Germany occupy Ukraine? | From 1941 to 1944 | From 1914 to 1918 | From 1939 to 1991 | From 1240 to 1480
exercise: What were people taken to Germany for forced labour called? | Ostarbeiter, «eastern workers» | Cossacks | Serfs of the Sich | Righteous Among the Nations, «good helpers»
exercise: Who were most of the people murdered at Babyn Yar in September 1941? | Jews of Kyiv | Polish soldiers | German prisoners | Crimean Tatars
exercise: Why were some Red Army fronts called «Ukrainian fronts»? | Because of where they fought | Because only Ukrainians served in them | Because they were led by hetmans | Because they flew the blue and yellow flag
exercise: Who won the Second World War in Europe? | The Allies, with the Soviet share fought by many peoples | Ukraine alone | Russia alone, with no help from anyone else at all, from start to end | Nobody won
exercise: What did UPA units do in Volhynia in 1943–1944? | Carried out mass killings of Polish civilians | Founded the United Nations | Freed Kyiv from the Nazis and held a great parade in the city | Built the Kyiv metro
exercise: Where were the Crimean Tatars deported in May 1944? | To Central Asia | To Canada | To the Carpathians | To Germany
exercise: What is Jamala's song «1944» about? | The deportation of the Crimean Tatars | The battle of Kruty and the student volunteers | The fall of the Berlin Wall | The flight to the Moon
exercise: What does the title Righteous Among the Nations mean? | A non-Jew who risked their life to save Jews | A champion of the Olympic Games who won gold for three nations | A Soviet general | A judge at the United Nations
exercise: Which church leader's network hid Jewish children? | Metropolitan Andrey Sheptytsky's | Patriarch Nikon's network in Moscow | Pope Gregory's, from far-off Rome | Prince Volodymyr the Great's network
exercise: When did the Red Army retake Kyiv? | 6 November 1943 | 22 June 1941, at dawn | 9 May 1945, Victory Day | 24 August 1991, Independence Day
exercise: Why was crossing the Dnipro so hard? | It is a very wide river, crossed under enemy fire | It was frozen solid all year round, so tanks slid on the ice | It was full of crocodiles | There was no river there
exercise: What was Soviet Ukraine in 1945 at the United Nations? | One of the 51 founding members | The UN's only founder | Not a member at all | The seat of the UN headquarters
exercise: What made the 1946–47 famine worse? | Grain exports after drought and war damage | Far too much rain in Kyiv that washed the fields away | The Chornobyl accident | Emigration to Brazil
exercise: Who transferred Crimea to the Ukrainian republic in 1954? | The Soviet government | The Ottoman sultan in Istanbul | The Central Rada in Kyiv | The United Nations in New York
test: Danylo says 1939 was a happy reunion of Ukraine. What would his grandmother add? | For many in the west it meant occupation, arrests and deportations | It was a happy party with no problems for anybody in any village or town | Nothing happened in 1939 | Poland gave the land as a present
test: «Ukraine suffered more than any country in the war, period». wise way to say it? | Ukraine was among the hardest-hit; ranking suffering doesn't help | Ukraine barely suffered at all | Only Ukraine suffered, and nobody else in the world lost anyone in the war | The war never reached Ukraine
test: Danylo says Babyn Yar was where Ukrainians were killed. What's the fuller truth? | It is first a place of the Holocaust, and others were murdered there too | Only Ukrainians died there, and no Jews were ever killed at that place | Nobody died there | It was a battle between armies
test: teacher says «Russia won the war», Danylo says «nah, Ukraine did». Who's right? | Neither alone: the Allies won, with millions of Ukrainians in the Red Army | Russia alone | Ukraine alone | Germany won
test: Danylo says the UPA were only heroes. The teacher's gran is Polish. What helps? | Name both its fight for independence and the killings in Volhynia, honestly | Never talk about it again | Say the Volhynia killings are made up by Polish writers after the war to blame Ukraine | Say the UPA fought for Hitler only
test: If Ukrainian and Polish historians refused to talk about Volhynia, what would likely happen? | Old anger would stay and the two peoples would trust each other less | They would become closer friends because they had nothing left to argue about | Nothing, history doesn't matter | The past would change
test: Danylo says only Ukrainians were deported by Stalin. What's the fix? | Many peoples were, including the whole Crimean Tatar people in 1944 | Yes, only Ukrainians | Nobody was ever deported | Only Americans were
test: «Ukrainians saved all the Jews», says Danylo. What's honest? | Many brave people saved Jews, some collaborated and most were afraid | Every single Ukrainian saved someone, and nobody ever helped the Nazis at all | Nobody saved anyone | Jews never lived in Ukraine
test: Danylo says Kyiv freed itself in 1943. What's more exact? | The Red Army retook it, many of its soldiers Ukrainians | Kyiv kids chased the Nazis out alone | The Americans landed in Kyiv | It was never occupied
test: Danylo bets the teacher Ukraine had a UN seat in 1945. teacher says no way. Who wins? | Danylo: Soviet Ukraine was a founding member | The teacher: Ukraine joined only in 2022 | Nobody, the UN didn't exist | The teacher: only Russia had seats
test: Danylo says there were three famines in Soviet Ukraine. teacher knows one. Who's right? | Danylo: 1921–23, 1932–33 and 1946–47 | The teacher: just one | Neither, there were none | Both wrong, there were ten
test: «Crimea was a gift, and Russia can't take gifts back lol». What's the real point? | Ukraine's borders, Crimea included, were recognised by treaties, even by Russia | Gifts can always be taken back | Crimea was never part of Ukraine, not even for one day in all its long history | Crimea belongs to Turkey by treaty
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the events at Babyn Yar in September nineteen hundred and forty-one? | Approximately thirty-three thousand Jews were murdered by Nazi forces over two days | A decisive battle was fought between two armies upon the outskirts of the city of Kyiv | The Red Army retook the city of Kyiv | The Crimean Tatars were deported to Central Asia
exam: Identify the action undertaken by the Soviet regime in respect of the Crimean Tatar people in May nineteen hundred and forty-four. | The deportation of the entire people to Central Asia | The grant of independence to Crimea | The construction of a nuclear power station | The admission of Crimea to the United Nations
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the status of Soviet Ukraine upon the foundation of the United Nations in nineteen hundred and forty-five. | It was one of fifty-one founding members, its foreign policy controlled from Moscow | It was the sole founder of the organisation | It was excluded from membership of the organisation until nineteen hundred and ninety-one | It hosted the headquarters of the organisation
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning the Ukrainian Insurgent Army is most consistent with the findings of historians. | It fought for independence, and certain of its units killed Polish civilians in Volhynia | It fought exclusively on the side of Nazi Germany | It never engaged any enemy | It was the principal army of the Western Allies
